% Stage 1: a helper variable becomes the expression it held, and everything
% that cannot be inlined is refused with a position.

### name: norm.inline.one-helper
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"TOTAL": {"kind": "column", "column": "total", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
LIMIT = 100; TOTAL > LIMIT
--- expect
(`total` > 100)
===
### name: norm.inline.chained-helpers
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
A = 1; B = A + 1; B + 1
--- expect
((1 + 1) + 1)
===
### name: norm.inline.helper-used-twice
--- note
Substitution is by AST node, so the expression appears once per use. SQL has
no place to put a shared temporary, and duplicating a pure expression is the
honest translation of one.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
A = 1 + 2; A * A
--- expect
((1 + 2) * (1 + 2))
===
### name: norm.list.comma-flattens-per-spec
--- note
spec §5.9: an operand with children and no scalar contributes each of them.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
A = ("x", "y"); S IN (A, "z")
--- expect
(((CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('x' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin) OR (CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('y' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)) OR (CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('z' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin))
===
### name: norm.trailing-semicolon
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 + 1;
--- expect
(1 + 1)
===
### name: norm.refuse.compound-assignment
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
A = 1; A += 1; A
--- error
E_SQL_ASSIGN 1:8
===
### name: norm.refuse.reassignment
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
A = 1; A = 2; A
--- error
E_SQL_ASSIGN 1:8
===
### name: norm.refuse.value-before-the-result-expression
--- note
1:3 is the + operator: a `bin` node takes its operator token's position, which
is the rule conformance/12-misuse.selt pins for the evaluator too.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 + 1; 2 + 2
--- error
E_SQL_ASSIGN 1:3
===
### name: norm.refuse.assignment-inside-an-expression
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
(A = 1) + 1
--- error
E_SQL_ASSIGN 1:2
===
### name: norm.refuse.indexed-and-whole-assignment-mixed
--- dialect
mariadb
--- note
1:9 is the [ — an assign takes its target's position, and an indexed target is
an `index` node positioned at its bracket.
--- source
A = 1; A[1] = 2; A
--- error
E_SQL_ASSIGN 1:9
===
### name: norm.refuse.non-constant-index-on-a-target
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"K": {"kind": "column", "column": "k", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
A[K] = 1; A
--- error
E_SQL_ASSIGN 1:3
===
### name: norm.refuse.unbound-variable
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
MYSTERY > 1
--- error
E_SQL_UNBOUND 1:1
===
### name: norm.inline.captures-at-assignment
--- note
The B inside A is the one that was in scope when A was written, which is what
SEL itself does — `A = B + 1; B = 2; A + B` with B = 10 evaluates to 13, not 5.
Inlining at the use instead would quietly rewrite the program. This case exists
so that "refuse a variable read before its assignment" is never reinstated as a
rule: there is nothing to refuse.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"B": {"kind": "column", "column": "b", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
A = B + 1; B = 2; A + B
--- expect
((`b` + 1) + 2)
===
### name: norm.value.negative-number-is-parenthesised
--- note
Otherwise unary minus in front of it makes `--`, which MariaDB reads as double
negation and PostgreSQL and SQLite read as the start of a line comment.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"LIM": {"kind": "value", "value": "-5", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
-LIM
--- expect
(-(-5))
